diagonal, so it has infinite dimensions: \code{\link[=finite_section]{finite_section()}} truncates it to
the frequencies \code{-n} to \code{n}, and \code{\link[=eigs]{eigs()}} runs on it directly and chooses the
truncation. With a real symbol (the derivative coefficients times \code{i^m} real)
and a real \code{V} (the multiplier conjugate-symmetric) it is self-adjoint by
construction, and of order at least one its spectrum is discrete, so the
certificate's residual bound is a distance to an eigenvalue of the operator on
the circle itself.

\code{kind = "chebyshev"} is the interval \verb{[-1, 1]}: a function is its Chebyshev
coefficients, the operator \verb{sum_m c_m u^(m) + a(x) u} is held in the
ultraspherical method (Olver and Townsend), mapping Chebyshev coefficients to
those of the ultraspherical basis \code{C^(p)} where it is banded, and \code{boundary}
gives one condition \code{u^(d)(+-1) = value} per order. \code{\link[=solve.linop]{solve()}} takes the
Chebyshev coefficients of the right-hand side and returns those of the
solution, and \code{\link[=eigs]{eigs()}} with \code{which = "smallest"} the eigenvalues of smallest
magnitude; both choose the number of coefficients and certify the exact
residual on the untruncated system. The boundary conditions define the
operator's domain: \code{\link[=adjoint]{adjoint()}} returns the adjoint operator with the adjoint
domain, by integration by parts (mixed conditions where the boundary form
requires them), and the operator records whether it is self-adjoint in L2.
For a self-adjoint \verb{-(k u')' + q u} with \code{k > 0} and homogeneous Dirichlet,
Neumann or Robin conditions, \code{eigs(..., method = "galerkin")} discretises the
weak form (natural conditions enter it as boundary terms, Dirichlet ones as
a basis that vanishes there) and returns upper bounds of the eigenvalues
(Courant-Fischer), lifted back to Chebyshev coefficients.

\code{kind = "hermite"} is the line, in the Hermite functions, where \code{x} and
\code{d/dx} are tridiagonal: \verb{sum_m c_m (d/dx)^m + V(x)} with \code{V} a polynomial given
by its monomial coefficients. \code{kind = "laguerre"} is the half line, in the
Laguerre functions \verb{exp(-x/2) L_n(x)}, where \code{x} is tridiagonal and the
Laguerre operator \verb{-(x u')' + x u / 4} is diagonal: \code{c} times that operator
plus a polynomial \code{V(x)}, with \code{derivative = c}. Both are banded operators on
coefficient sequences, truncated and certified as on the circle.
